course: euphemisms
lesson: 42
title: Olympiad: reading between the lines ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Olympiad texts pack many soft words into one passage: a house advert, a school report, a news story or a death notice, and you must translate every phrase before you answer.
explain: Read like a detective: an advert that says "compact" and "needs some TLC" is telling you small and falling apart, and a report that says "sociable" and "a work in progress" is telling you chatty and not good yet.
explain: Some questions ask you to put events in order, some to count, some to say how big or bad a thing really is; turn each phrase into plain words first, then solve.
explain: Spin goes both ways: officials make bad news sound small ("efficiency savings" for cuts), while boasters and grumblers make things sound huge ("it took forever"), so the real size is in the plain facts.
explain: Picture a letter home that says "Josh is a natural leader and an enthusiastic contributor": his teacher is gently telling his parents that Josh is bossy and shouts out answers.
fact: "Compact" in a house advert means small, and "deceptively spacious" usually means small too.
fact: "Needs some TLC" means a place is in bad condition and needs repairs; TLC stands for "tender loving care".
fact: "Pre-loved" means second-hand, and "fun-size" means small.
fact: "Price adjustment" usually means the price went up.
fact: On a school report, "natural leader" can mean bossy and "enthusiastic contributor" can mean shouts out answers without waiting.
fact: "Sociable" on a report often means talks too much in class, and "has made some progress" often means very little progress.
fact: "Needs encouragement" on a report usually means lazy, and "emerging" means well below the expected level.
fact: "Efficiency savings" means budget cuts; "streamlining" and "rightsizing" mean cutting jobs.
fact: "Headwinds" in business means problems that hurt sales.
fact: "Lessons have been learned" is the official way of admitting a failure.
fact: "We are reviewing our options" often means nothing has been decided yet.
fact: "A good day to bury bad news" means a day when some bigger story will stop people noticing your bad news.
fact: "A long illness" in a death notice usually means cancer or another slow disease, and "slipped away" means died quietly.
fact: "Slipped away" can also mean left a place quietly, so the rest of the sentence tells you which meaning is meant.
fact: "Didn't make it" means died, usually after an accident or illness.
fact: "A frank exchange of views" and "a robust discussion" mean an angry argument; "strategic withdrawal" means a retreat.
fact: "Not our finest hour" and "a learning experience" mean a failure; "a mixed bag" means partly good and partly bad.
fact: "Shrinkage" is shop talk for goods lost to theft.
fact: "Not ideal" and "a bit of a pickle" are British understatement for something bad, and "a funny turn" can be a faint or a small stroke.
fact: A hyperbole number such as "a million times" or "forever" is never the number to count; the real number in the text is.
exercise: Advert: "Compact, deceptively spacious cottage. Needs some TLC. Pre-loved furniture included." What is the cottage really like? | Small, in bad repair, with second-hand furniture | Large and brand new, with modern new furniture | Small but freshly repaired, with new furniture | Big and in bad repair, with fine antique furniture
exercise: Report: "Ruby is sociable, a natural leader, and has made some progress in maths." What does it really say? | Ruby chats, is bossy, and is a little better at maths | Ruby is quiet, shy and very good at maths | Ruby chats, is kind and is top in maths | Ruby is lonely, bossy and does no maths
exercise: Which report line is plain praise with no hidden meaning? | "Reads quickly and accurately." | "Needs encouragement in maths." | "Has a strong personality." | "Is emerging in writing."
exercise: "'Lessons have been learned,' said the boss on Friday, after Monday's 'streamlining', which followed a year of 'headwinds'." What is the right order? | Sales suffered, jobs were cut, the boss admitted failure | Jobs were cut, sales suffered, the boss admitted failure | The boss admitted failure, sales suffered, jobs were cut | Sales rose, jobs were added, the boss had a party
exercise: Council: "Due to efficiency savings, the library will open on fewer days." What really happened? | The council cut the library's money | New computers saved the staff time | The library got extra money this year | The library will now be open for longer
exercise: Sign: "Price adjustment: bus fare from £2 to £3." What is the sign softening? | A rise of £1 | A cut of £1 | A rise of £3 | No change at all
exercise: Death notice: "After a long illness, Mrs Kaur slipped away peacefully on Sunday, aged 89." Which is true? | She died quietly on Sunday after being ill a long time | She left hospital quietly on Sunday, cured at last | She died suddenly on Sunday after a fall | She went quietly on holiday on Sunday, aged 89
exercise: "Two climbers were hurt: one didn't make it, the other is laid up in hospital." How many climbers are alive? | One | Two | None | Both of them
exercise: Which line is British understatement? | "Well, that's not ideal," as the kitchen floods | "Best day ever!!!" after a sunny picnic | "I've waited a million years," after a short wait | "We're all doomed!" when the bus is late
exercise: Minister: "We are reviewing our options." A reporter asks: "So has anything been decided?" What is the honest answer? | No, nothing yet | Yes, the plan is decided | Yes, a new law has been passed | No, the plan has been cancelled
exercise: Army report: "After a robust discussion, the general ordered a strategic withdrawal." What happened? | After an angry argument, the army retreated | After a calm chat, the army attacked | After an angry argument, the army attacked first | After a calm chat, the army retreated
exercise: Odd one out: "headwinds", "a learning experience", "not our finest hour", "efficiency gains". | "Efficiency gains": fewer staff, not a failure | "Headwinds": good luck for the business | "A learning experience": a good school trip | "Not our finest hour": a very late start
exercise: Coach: "We've lost a million games! Sunday's 4–0 defeat was not our finest hour, and Tuesday's 3–1 defeat was a learning experience." How many goals did the team let in over the two games? | 7 | 5 | 8 | 1,000,000
exercise: A firm announces job cuts on the morning of a huge royal wedding. Why might it choose that day? | Everyone is watching the wedding, so few will notice | Weddings make every firm rich, so it can easily afford it | It is a lucky day for business in the calendar | Newspapers are not printed on wedding days
exercise: Report: "Leo is emerging in reading." His mum says, "So he's rising to the top!" Is she right? | No: emerging means well below the expected level | Yes: emerging means he is coming top of the class | Yes: it means he reads aloud very well | No: it means he has not started reading
exercise: "Our hamster was laid to rest in the garden; the vet had put him to sleep; he'd been off colour for weeks." What is the real order? | Unwell, the vet ended his life, buried | Buried, the vet ended his life, unwell | The vet ended his life, unwell, buried | Unwell, buried, the vet ended his life
exercise: The estate agent says "deceptively spacious". Jas says, "So it's bigger than it looks!" What usually turns out? | It is small | It is a huge castle | It is bigger inside than out | It has no rooms at all
exercise: Letter A: "Kai has a strong personality." Letter B: "Kai is kind and helps others." Which letter hides a criticism? | Letter A | Letter B | Both letters | Neither letter
exercise: Shop board: "Pre-loved jeans £5. Fun-size chocolate bars 3 for £1." What is true? | The jeans are second-hand and the bars are small | The jeans are new and the bars are huge | The jeans are well loved, and the bars are full-size | The jeans are free and the bars are funny
exercise: News: "There were disturbances in the city, and police used less-lethal weapons." What happened? | There were riots, and police used weapons such as rubber bullets | There was noise, and the police used no weapons at all | There was a parade, and police used rubber bullets | There were riots, and police used deadly guns
test: "lowkey sus, the ad says cosy, compact, needs TLC, and it's £900 a month lol" What is the flat really like? | Small and in need of repairs | Big and brand new | Cheap and perfect | Warm and very large
test: Teacher wrote "Mia is sociable", no wait, she crossed it out and wrote "Mia talks over others". What does the final report say? | Mia talks over others in class | Mia makes friends easily in class | Mia is quiet and shy in class | Mia is top of the class in maths
test: Gran on the phone: "Just had a funny turn, love, nothing to worry about, don't tell your mum." What should you do? | Tell an adult straight away: a funny turn can be serious | Keep it secret, as Gran asked you to | Laugh: it was just a joke | Wait until next week
test: Dad, very calm: "Bit of a pickle, love. The car's in the river." How bad is it really? | Very bad, though he made it sound small | Not bad at all, just a small problem | Funny and harmless, like a prank | A jar of pickles fell in the river
test: "the queue at the servo was a mile long, took forever, I waited like 20 min" (a servo is a petrol station). How long did he wait? | About 20 minutes | Forever | As long as a mile | 20 hours
test: News: "The minister misspoke, was relieved of his duties, and his party said lessons had been learned." What happened to the minister in the end? | He lost his job | He was promoted | He went on holiday | He won an award
test: Shop sign: "Due to shrinkage, prices have been adjusted." What is the shop blaming for the price rise? | Goods being stolen | Clothes shrinking in the wash | Customers getting thinner | A cold winter
test: "Mam's tamping (well cross): my report says I'm a work in progress and not working to my full potential." Why is Mam cross? | Not good yet, and lazy | It says he is brilliant at everything | It says he must move school | It says he has lost all his books
test: "Chur bro, the firm had headwinds, now it's rightsizing. Uncle reckons his job's safe as." Should Uncle be so sure? | No: rightsizing means cutting jobs | Yes: rightsizing means hiring more people | Yes: headwinds help a business grow | No: it means the firm is moving
test: Which sentence tells you that someone died? | "After a long illness, Dad slipped away." | "Dad slipped away early from the party." | "Dad slipped away to make a quick phone call." | "Dad slipped on the ice and laughed."
test: Odd one out in an advert: "pre-loved bike", "needs some TLC", "compact frame", "brand-new tyres". | "Brand-new tyres": plain and good | "Pre-loved bike": loved by a famous rider | "Compact frame": an extra large frame | "Needs some TLC": in perfect shape
test: Footy: "not our finest hour" after a 0–6 defeat, "a mixed bag" after a 2–2 draw, "a learning experience" after a 1–5 defeat. Which game was the worst loss? | The 0–6 defeat | The 2–2 draw | The 1–5 defeat | All equally bad
exam: An advertisement describes a dwelling as "compact" and requiring "some TLC". Determine the condition so described. | A small dwelling in poor repair | A spacious dwelling in excellent repair | A small dwelling recently renovated | A spacious dwelling in poor repair
exam: A school report states that a pupil is "emerging" in reading. Identify the plain meaning of this statement. | The pupil is well below the expected level | The pupil is well above the expected level | The pupil is at exactly the expected level | The pupil has not yet begun reading lessons
exam: A report states: "Following headwinds in spring, efficiency savings were made in summer; in autumn the board said lessons had been learned." Identify what occurred in summer. | Spending was cut | Sales rose markedly | The board admitted failure | Problems first hurt sales
exam: Identify the instance of British understatement among the following. | "Not ideal", said of a flooded kitchen | "Forever", said of a ten-minute wait | "A ton", said of a heavy shopping bag | "Starving", said an hour before lunch
